we can talk now to the chair of global  public health at edinburgh university  professor  debbie srider thank you so much for  being on the programme  um the prime minister of course laying  out his plan  for look now and easing tomorrow how  cautious do you think he needs to be and  what do you think can happen  well the thing to say is there's light  ahead and we should be optimistic  because of the vaccines as a medium to  long-term way out of this if we look at  israel we are seeing that once they have  gotten down to vaccinating  those in their 20s and their 30s they're  seeing actually  that numbers are falling and so there is  a way to suppress this virus  and to stop severe disease with our  vaccines however  we are far way off from vaccinating  people in those age groups  hopefully as the health secretary said  by the summer so the question is how do  we ease restrictions in a one-way  process we don't see a third wave over  the next few months while the vaccine's  rolled out  and that requires a strong test trace  isolate system  especially supporting people in  isolating who are testing positive as  well as mass testing to really hit  higher prevalence areas  younger children in scotland and wales  are going to be returning to school  tomorrow return to the classroom  tomorrow it feels like in england the  ambition is to get  all schools back to all pupils on the  8th of march  who do you think it's got the right  approach well  i'm obviously biased because i advise  the scottish government  on their education policies and so i  would agree what we've done  here which is tomorrow having the  younger kids go back and to assess after  several weeks  what has been the impact on transmission  what have we seen in schools  and we were learning from denmark who  are ahead of us several weeks and who  also  have the new variant the new variant is  creating uncertainty which means we need  to go more cautiously  so there is a huge risk with bringing  back all kids at the same time and then  having to shut schools again and we're  trying here to move slowly to move  cautiously and  get kids back as many kids as possible  but in a slow  staged phased way and with testing the  other thing i'd mention and it's an easy  win  for i think england is in scotland we  have allowed children  under the age of 12 to play outside in  groups up to 13.  only two adults can be there but  children can play in groups so we've  seen children doing sledding tennis  football  out in parks and this gives kind of  outdoor play and also a form of kind of  physical education  and that is an easy win because we know  transmission outside is minimal and the  mental health benefits are huge  um you talk about the um vaccine rollout  going well  and we heard just a moment ago from  william hague who said that once  everyone over 50 is vaccinated the top  nine priority groups which we're  expecting by mid-april  he says there wouldn't be much  justification for keeping restrictions  on people  once this group is protected given they  make up 99  of deaths of covid it does feel to many  people i suppose that  there is a good case for lifting  restrictions  yeah i think we all want to lift  restrictions as quickly as possible  i mean i'm craving going out to a party  or somewhere outside my home but i think  the thing to remember is what gets us  with this virus  is the hospitalization rate and how many  people are susceptible  and the hospitalizations do go down to  people in their 30s and 40s  which means that is what we're really  going after of course the deaths but  also the hospitals being full and not  being able to provide  routine care for all the things we need  the nhs for and so i'd say  of course we want to lift restrictions  but it has to be down to the prevalence  of the virus have we got it low enough  to testing positivity are we testing  enough finding cases  as well as are we actually vaccinating  those most at risk of hospitalization  and learning from other countries such  as israel they once they vaccinated  their over 60s they found a lot of under  60s in hospitals too  so we have to be kind of realistic of  what it's going to look like for the  next few months  and what does the data show on the  impact of the vaccine are we starting to  get any indication yet that it's  having an impact yes i think we're  definitely seeing a reduction in  deaths in older age groups you know  people in over 80 since that group has  been covered  over 70s and we know i mean to tell  people i mean a year ago we didn't even  think we'd have a vaccine and now we  have several  and the effectiveness is astonishing  especially of the mrna ones  and so we are in a strong position to  stop severe disease  and even it seems like glimmers of hope  for stopping transmission  so i think where we're heading with this  you know people say when is the pandemic  going to end  well there's a real chance to drive the  numbers super low using vaccines using  outbreak response  and then it's time for britain and along  the way to look across the world and say  the pandemic will only be over if we  look at  poor countries fragile states you know  130 countries don't have any vaccines at  all right now  and so it's a dual responsibility both  at home but also abroad  i mean you mentioned 130 countries  without any vaccine which clearly has  big implications for the global fight  against covet  but at the same time many people in the  uk would say that we shouldn't give  vaccines away until  every adult has had at least one dose  what do you think the right thing to do  would be  this is a really tricky one and i think  it comes down to three arguments why we  have to be  vaccinating at home but also looking  abroad and the first is the moral one  is it right to be vaccinating people in  their 20s and 30s here when you have  health workers dying  in africa because they can't get access  to a vaccine the second one is actually  self-interest  we know that this virus will mutate we  have seen mutations in south africa and  in brazil  which can reduce the effectiveness of  our vaccines  so if we want to be open to the world  and have people coming in  then we need to make sure we're actually  you know watching out for variants  coming in which will emerge if there's  uncontrolled spread of the virus in  other places and the third is we are  seeing russia and china  donating their vaccines to low-income  countries  for clear geopolitical reasons and so  we've heard you know now with g7  um going on that actually you know there  is concern about what will it mean for  the influence of european and north  american countries  if china and russia are seen as being  the most reliable support rather than  western world okay debbie schroeder  thank you very much professor devi  shredder there
 

